WEBVTT

1
00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.860
I think we will get started.

2
00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:00.920
So we're going to head back into public submissions on the

3
00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:04.560
Conservation Amendment Bill, and our first

4
00:01:04.621 --> 00:01:08.400
submitter today is Education Outdoors New Zealand,

5
00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:12.760
who are joining us online. Welcome, Fiona. We can see you there.

6
00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:17.060
You've got 15 minutes with us this morning, so we'll take your submission as

7
00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.220
read, but we'll hand over to you for some opening remarks, and then open up for

8
00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:21.500
questions. So over to you.

9
00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:26.160
Tena koutou katoa. Thanks for the opportunity to speak to you today.

10
00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:30.300
I'm Fiona MacDonald, tukangunga, ngā otutao yā hau.

11
00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:34.060
I'm the Chief Executive for Education Outdoors New Zealand, or EONS.

12
00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:38.340
EONS supports schools, careers, teachers, and outdoor

13
00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:42.180
providers deliver safe, high-quality outdoor education and

14
00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:45.280
education outside the classroom across Aotearoa New Zealand.

15
00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:49.940
We're here today because public conservation land is one of the most important

16
00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:51.420
classrooms in the country.

17
00:01:51.970 --> 00:01:52.160
Mm.

18
00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:56.179
It's where young people learn to read the weather, the rivers.

19
00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:56.720
Mm.

20
00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:00.300
It's where they learn to walk with others, make decisions,

21
00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:04.180
manage uncertainty, listen to local stories, and understand

22
00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:07.860
ecosystem systems, and experience their own

23
00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:11.080
capabilities. It's where many ākonga

24
00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:15.660
will sleep in a tent for the first time, cross a river, hear native

25
00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:19.380
birds in their habitat, see stars away from city lights, and

26
00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.020
understand that conservation and restoration is not an abstract idea.

27
00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:24.380
It's something they can participate in.

28
00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:28.880
And that's why EONS' message today, reinforcing our

29
00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:32.820
submission, is really simple. The Conservation Act must place

30
00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:36.790
conservation first. Education, recreation, and

31
00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:40.610
public access and enjoyment clearly sit in behind that and should

32
00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:42.570
always be consistent with conservation.

33
00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:47.360
And commercial activity should only occur where it supports and does not

34
00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:50.480
limit, undermine, or displace those first two purposes.

35
00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:53.320
We're not opposed to commercial activity.

36
00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:57.700
Well-managed tourism and appropriate concessions can definitely help people

37
00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:00.320
access and appreciate special places.

38
00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:04.200
But the Act must be very clear about the order of priorities.

39
00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:08.860
Public conservation land shouldn't be treated primarily as an

40
00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:12.900
economic development platform. If conservation is weakened,

41
00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:16.360
then everything that's built on it is weakened, too; outdoor education,

42
00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:20.240
recreation, cultural connection, community wellbeing, and the

43
00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:21.080
visitor economy.

44
00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:25.800
People don't come to Aotearoa New Zealand to see dammed rivers,

45
00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:29.880
industrialized wild places, demodiversity ones, come for the

46
00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:33.690
mountains, the amazing views, wildlife, culture and

47
00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:37.670
adventure, story, and the experience of being in places that feel

48
00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:41.600
alive. The economic benefit that comes from tourism depends

49
00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:44.460
on health and integrity of nature.

50
00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:49.080
For schools, the environment matters in very practical ways.

51
00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:53.340
A teacher taking a group into a conservation area is not just

52
00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:57.150
taking students for a walk. They may be there to teach outdoor

53
00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:01.049
education through decision-making, risk awareness, leadership,

54
00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:04.910
and care for place. They might be teaching science through direct

55
00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:08.820
observation of ecosystems, PE through movement and challenge, or

56
00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:09.310
geography

57
00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:12.040
through landforms and human impact.

58
00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:16.690
They may be supporting te reo Maori, mātauranga Māori through place names,

59
00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:20.620
pūrākau, whakapapa, and relationships with whenua and

60
00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:20.921
wai.

61
00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:25.580
Teaching citizenship by helping young people understand public good,

62
00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:29.960
shared responsibility, and the long-term consequences of the decisions we

63
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.040
make about places.

64
00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:36.080
That learning can't be replicated fully in a classroom, on a screen, or

65
00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:39.950
through a worksheet. People learn differently when they're

66
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:43.260
in place. They notice things. They ask different questions.

67
00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:47.420
They understand scale, interdependence, and consequence in a

68
00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:50.300
way that's impossible to recreate indoors.

69
00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:55.060
Healthy, flourishing environments support healthy, flourishing

70
00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:58.970
people. And the opposite is true when

71
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.820
young people grow up with reduced access to healthy

72
00:05:01.829 --> 00:05:05.660
places. We lose an education pathway

73
00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:09.430
into environmental understanding, resilience, cultural connection,

74
00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:10.700
confidence, and care.

75
00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:15.480
And that's why EONS is asking the committee to apply an intergenerational

76
00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:16.680
test to this act.

77
00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.790
The question should not be what economic use can we enable now?

78
00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:25.170
The question should be, what will future generations inherit because

79
00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:26.750
of the decisions we make today?

80
00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:30.520
Will they inherit a healthier ecosystem?

81
00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:34.640
Will they inherit access? Will they inherit places where they can still learn,

82
00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:38.940
explore, listen, observe, challenge themselves, and develop

83
00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:40.530
a lifelong ethic of care?

84
00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:44.730
Will mana whenua relationships with place be upheld and strengthened?

85
00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:49.180
Will both mātauranga Māori and ecological science inform decisions?

86
00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:53.990
And will public conservation land still feel like public conservation

87
00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:54.380
land?

88
00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:59.340
If the answer's uncertain, the Act should take a proactive path.

89
00:06:00.664 --> 00:06:03.224
And protect conservation values.

90
00:06:04.224 --> 00:06:07.024
We do recognise that the system needs to be modernised.

91
00:06:07.564 --> 00:06:09.944
Processes can be slow, complex, and frustrating.

92
00:06:10.784 --> 00:06:14.424
So we support any reform that improves clarity,

93
00:06:14.504 --> 00:06:18.444
efficiency, consistency and good decision-making, but it must

94
00:06:18.484 --> 00:06:22.104
improve how the conservation system works, not change why it

95
00:06:22.144 --> 00:06:22.784
exists.

96
00:06:23.704 --> 00:06:27.684
And our concern is with wording such as "recognise economic

97
00:06:27.924 --> 00:06:31.594
opportunities" and "enable the use and development to the greatest

98
00:06:31.664 --> 00:06:32.484
possible extent."

99
00:06:33.304 --> 00:06:35.264
That's creating the wrong pull on the system.

100
00:06:36.004 --> 00:06:39.954
It risks asking DOC decision-makers to look for development

101
00:06:40.044 --> 00:06:43.944
pathways rather than first asking what the place needs in

102
00:06:44.004 --> 00:06:47.494
order to remain healthy or to be restored to health, be

103
00:06:47.564 --> 00:06:50.844
resilient in the future, and be available for public good for future

104
00:06:50.904 --> 00:06:51.664
generations.

105
00:06:54.204 --> 00:06:58.064
Conservation land's not empty land waiting for higher economic use.

106
00:06:58.484 --> 00:06:59.984
It already has immense value.

107
00:07:01.144 --> 00:07:04.874
It holds biodiversity, whakapapa, history, identity,

108
00:07:05.444 --> 00:07:09.264
quiet and dark skies, wildlife, mahinga kai,

109
00:07:09.344 --> 00:07:13.164
stories and memories. And lots of those are values that can't be rebuilt

110
00:07:13.244 --> 00:07:16.604
once they're lost. They can't be offset or have a price put on them.

111
00:07:17.424 --> 00:07:21.344
And therefore, we really believe that the Act needs to make the

112
00:07:21.364 --> 00:07:25.164
hierarchy unmistakable. Protect and enhance natural,

113
00:07:25.284 --> 00:07:27.424
cultural, and historic values first.

114
00:07:28.024 --> 00:07:31.504
Secondly, support education, public access, responsible

115
00:07:31.544 --> 00:07:34.554
recreation, and public enjoyment where they're consistent with

116
00:07:34.564 --> 00:07:38.344
conservation. And thirdly, allow tourism, commercial

117
00:07:38.424 --> 00:07:42.084
activity, and economic uses only where they

118
00:07:42.264 --> 00:07:46.034
are demonstratively consistent with conservation and do not

119
00:07:46.064 --> 00:07:49.794
materially compromise the above values.

120
00:07:51.464 --> 00:07:53.624
The hierarchy is not anti-tourism.

121
00:07:53.984 --> 00:07:56.344
It's the foundation for long-term tourism.

122
00:07:57.964 --> 00:08:01.934
EONZ also asks the committee to give stronger attention to education as

123
00:08:01.964 --> 00:08:04.744
a public good outcome for conservation land.

124
00:08:05.984 --> 00:08:09.313
Outdoor education is one of the ways that young people become future

125
00:08:09.484 --> 00:08:10.744
conservation advocates.

126
00:08:11.904 --> 00:08:15.184
When a student's helped planting with track care

127
00:08:15.244 --> 00:08:18.724
biosecurity or low-impact travel, they're not just

128
00:08:18.824 --> 00:08:22.224
visiting, they're learning stewardship and tiakitanga.

129
00:08:22.724 --> 00:08:26.624
They're learning that the outdoors is not simply a place to consume, but a

130
00:08:26.664 --> 00:08:30.344
place to understand, respect, care for, and return to.

131
00:08:31.564 --> 00:08:35.504
And these young people, they're going to be our future teachers, parents,

132
00:08:35.644 --> 00:08:39.024
guides, conservation volunteers, decision-makers,

133
00:08:39.595 --> 00:08:41.804
community, and mana whenua leaders.

134
00:08:42.444 --> 00:08:46.344
If we want a society that supports conservation, young people need

135
00:08:46.404 --> 00:08:50.024
meaningful opportunities to experience conservation land.

136
00:08:51.664 --> 00:08:55.624
So we'd like to see the removal of unnecessary barriers for

137
00:08:55.684 --> 00:08:57.164
not-for-profit outdoor education.

138
00:08:57.884 --> 00:09:01.484
Schools can undertake outdoor education on public conservation land,

139
00:09:02.584 --> 00:09:06.004
rightly exempt from concessions.

140
00:09:06.064 --> 00:09:10.004
But not-for-profit outdoor education providers delivering similar education

141
00:09:10.504 --> 00:09:14.324
experiences, often in partnership with schools, are still required to hold

142
00:09:14.364 --> 00:09:14.984
concessions.

143
00:09:16.044 --> 00:09:19.664
The learning's similar, the students are similar, the places are similar, so it's

144
00:09:19.744 --> 00:09:21.604
hard to justify the distinction.

145
00:09:22.784 --> 00:09:26.664
And schools are increasingly reliant on support of these specialist outdoor

146
00:09:26.744 --> 00:09:30.344
providers to deliver more complex outdoor education safely.

147
00:09:31.344 --> 00:09:34.984
They're not commercial tourism operators seeking

148
00:09:35.364 --> 00:09:37.144
private gain from conservation land.

149
00:09:37.633 --> 00:09:40.764
They're often charitable trusts or tertiary education

150
00:09:40.804 --> 00:09:44.404
organisations whose purpose is education, youth development, and

151
00:09:44.484 --> 00:09:48.344
wellbeing. And when concession costs, uncertainty, delays,

152
00:09:48.424 --> 00:09:51.984
or conditions on those programs make them less

153
00:09:52.024 --> 00:09:54.464
viable, the result's not better conservation.

154
00:09:55.264 --> 00:09:57.364
It's fewer young people learning in te taiao.

155
00:09:59.924 --> 00:10:03.724
So we ask the committee to recommend adding outdoor education to the list of

156
00:10:03.804 --> 00:10:07.564
activities that can be carried out on conservation land without a consent,

157
00:10:08.244 --> 00:10:12.224
where that activity is delivered by a not-for-profit organisation.

158
00:10:14.944 --> 00:10:18.784
We also would ask the committee to ensure Te Tiriti o

159
00:10:18.864 --> 00:10:22.454
Waitangi is explicit in the Act and in the National

160
00:10:22.524 --> 00:10:26.224
Conservation Statement. Mana whenua relationships with

161
00:10:26.344 --> 00:10:29.484
place shouldn't be treated as a consultation step.

162
00:10:30.564 --> 00:10:34.054
They must be part of decision-making from the beginning, and the National

163
00:10:34.124 --> 00:10:36.074
Conservation Policy Statement should make

164
00:10:36.884 --> 00:10:40.344
or should provide clear direction for mana whenua decision-making,

165
00:10:40.884 --> 00:10:44.804
iwi and hapū relationships with place, tikanga-led approaches to access and

166
00:10:44.844 --> 00:10:48.344
learning, and the use of mātauranga Māori alongside ecological

167
00:10:48.354 --> 00:10:51.514
science. That's not separate from outdoor education, it's

168
00:10:51.924 --> 00:10:55.704
central. When young people learn in places shaped by mana whenua

169
00:10:55.724 --> 00:10:59.524
knowledge, histories, and tikanga, they're more likely to

170
00:10:59.564 --> 00:11:03.544
develop respect, care, responsibility, and long-term commitment

171
00:11:03.604 --> 00:11:04.214
to te taiao,

172
00:11:05.024 --> 00:11:05.664
the environment.

173
00:11:06.944 --> 00:11:10.784
And finally, we ask that access for students, schools, kura,

174
00:11:11.164 --> 00:11:13.064
youth organisations, and not-for-profit

175
00:11:14.144 --> 00:11:16.124
outdoor education providers is protected.

176
00:11:17.144 --> 00:11:18.264
Access to te taiao,

177
00:11:19.404 --> 00:11:22.764
the environment and conservation estate is an equity issue.

178
00:11:23.164 --> 00:11:25.904
It's a wellbeing issue, and it's an education issue.

179
00:11:26.184 --> 00:11:30.044
And conservation land should never become a

180
00:11:30.084 --> 00:11:33.314
consumer experience available to only those who can afford it.

181
00:11:34.804 --> 00:11:35.544
So in closing,

182
00:11:36.344 --> 00:11:40.014
we ask the committee to amend the bill so that

183
00:11:40.064 --> 00:11:43.794
conservation remains the primary purpose for the conservation system.

184
00:11:44.684 --> 00:11:48.524
We ask that education is recognised as a core public

185
00:11:48.604 --> 00:11:49.244
good outcome,

186
00:11:50.844 --> 00:11:54.564
that commercial activity is clearly subordinate and is only

187
00:11:54.724 --> 00:11:58.464
enabled when it generally supports conservation

188
00:11:58.564 --> 00:11:59.154
outcomes,

189
00:12:00.164 --> 00:12:03.964
that unnecessary barriers, such as requiring concessions for outdoor

190
00:12:04.084 --> 00:12:07.384
education providers, are removed So there's

191
00:12:07.424 --> 00:12:11.144
consistency between schools and not-for-profit after-education

192
00:12:11.604 --> 00:12:15.284
providers. And we ask the committee to take the, a long view.

193
00:12:16.224 --> 00:12:20.074
The future of conservation in Aotearoa, uh, will not be secured only

194
00:12:20.104 --> 00:12:22.444
by rules, plans, and concessions.

195
00:12:22.484 --> 00:12:26.074
It's also secured by young people who know and love these places,

196
00:12:26.774 --> 00:12:30.644
understand their responsibilities, and can imagine themselves as

197
00:12:30.684 --> 00:12:34.504
kaitiaki, educators, advocates, and careful users

198
00:12:34.564 --> 00:12:38.244
of te— te taiao. Healthy places grow

199
00:12:38.324 --> 00:12:41.754
healthy people. Healthy people are more likely to care for healthy

200
00:12:41.844 --> 00:12:45.684
places, and the Conservation Act should protect that relationship

201
00:12:45.724 --> 00:12:48.004
for all our mokopuna into the future.

202
00:12:48.704 --> 00:12:50.244
Thank you

203
00:12:52.684 --> 00:12:56.164
very much, Fiona. Um, we've just got time for a couple of questions.

204
00:12:56.204 --> 00:12:57.344
We'll start with Celia.

205
00:12:57.904 --> 00:12:59.664
Thank you very much. Thank you, Fiona.

206
00:12:59.684 --> 00:13:00.394
You're welcome.

207
00:13:00.394 --> 00:13:03.724
I'm just going to narrow down on the concession question,

208
00:13:04.324 --> 00:13:08.124
and, um, the cost of concessions if you've

209
00:13:08.164 --> 00:13:11.884
got an external provider. Do you think that that could, um,

210
00:13:12.064 --> 00:13:15.964
impact the safety of children because schools and parents

211
00:13:16.024 --> 00:13:19.264
choose the cheaper option rather than the safer option?

212
00:13:19.744 --> 00:13:23.324
Yes, that's definitely one of our concerns, and also

213
00:13:23.384 --> 00:13:27.254
it's often, uh, the, um, conditions that are

214
00:13:27.254 --> 00:13:31.124
put around the concession, so they're only allowed to take a certain number

215
00:13:31.224 --> 00:13:35.004
of students, which limits the viability of running that

216
00:13:35.044 --> 00:13:38.844
program, and that's one of the concession requirements that

217
00:13:38.884 --> 00:13:40.144
schools don't have to face.

218
00:13:41.264 --> 00:13:41.584
Thank you.

219
00:13:42.134 --> 00:13:45.964
So we want to reduce down those barriers so schools and students, uh, are

220
00:13:46.044 --> 00:13:48.854
enabled to get out there in the safest possible way.

221
00:13:50.284 --> 00:13:51.744
Thank you. Priyanka.

222
00:13:52.864 --> 00:13:53.404
Kia ora.

223
00:13:54.084 --> 00:13:57.144
Kia ora. Um, uh, thanks for your submission.

224
00:13:57.704 --> 00:14:01.464
I just have a question around the point that you've made, um,

225
00:14:01.684 --> 00:14:04.524
y-you've said that not all commercial activity is harmful, but the bill's

226
00:14:04.544 --> 00:14:08.124
direction's too broad, and you've got a, um, a few

227
00:14:08.224 --> 00:14:11.414
suggestions in the table around a, a hierarchy,

228
00:14:12.024 --> 00:14:15.464
um, and, uh, the bit that I want to ask you about

229
00:14:16.044 --> 00:14:19.764
is your point to remove or substan- substantially redraft

230
00:14:20.324 --> 00:14:24.004
clauses that require economic opportunities to be recognized and

231
00:14:24.084 --> 00:14:27.944
enabled to the greatest extent practicable.

232
00:14:27.953 --> 00:14:31.794
Um, would removing the greatest extent practicable along

233
00:14:31.824 --> 00:14:35.784
with the other amendments that you've suggested be enough, or were you

234
00:14:35.824 --> 00:14:39.244
thinking of something else in terms of redrafting specific

235
00:14:39.304 --> 00:14:39.884
clauses?

236
00:14:40.364 --> 00:14:44.084
Um, I think that would certainly go a ways towards, um,

237
00:14:44.304 --> 00:14:47.804
helping. What we're really concerned about is making sure

238
00:14:47.864 --> 00:14:51.654
that the priority really stays in that conservation

239
00:14:52.024 --> 00:14:55.824
space, and those are the key drivers around is

240
00:14:55.884 --> 00:14:59.364
it, um, is this activity going to improve the health,

241
00:14:59.884 --> 00:15:02.864
support restoration if that needs to happen?

242
00:15:03.364 --> 00:15:07.244
Um, so anything that can shift and make sure the priority stays with

243
00:15:07.284 --> 00:15:11.233
conservation, then with education, recreation, and public access,

244
00:15:11.664 --> 00:15:15.173
and lastly, enabling commercial

245
00:15:15.264 --> 00:15:18.204
activity, whether that's tourism or other activities.

246
00:15:19.644 --> 00:15:19.924
Thank you.

247
00:15:20.984 --> 00:15:23.364
Thank you. Look, we'll take one last question from Grant McCollum.

248
00:15:23.424 --> 00:15:25.404
