574
00:33:33.386 --> 00:33:33.826
Thanks. Thank you.

575
00:33:34.786 --> 00:33:38.006
Tēnā koutou katoa. My name's Kaitlin Owens. This is Jamie Fowler.

576
00:33:38.726 --> 00:33:41.016
Thank you for the opportunity to speak to you today about the Conservation

577
00:33:41.066 --> 00:33:44.586
Amendment Bill. We want to start by saying that WWF does not

578
00:33:44.646 --> 00:33:46.466
support the bill in its current form.

579
00:33:47.686 --> 00:33:51.086
Public conservation land and waters belong to the people of Aotearoa New Zealand.

580
00:33:51.366 --> 00:33:55.146
They protect some of our most important and irreplaceable natural places.

581
00:33:55.186 --> 00:33:58.326
They provide a home for threatened species and help sustain the natural environment

582
00:33:58.366 --> 00:33:59.826
that underpins so much of our economy.

583
00:34:00.546 --> 00:34:02.926
And let's face it, they're also what draw people in from all around the world.

584
00:34:03.746 --> 00:34:05.326
But these places are about more than that.

585
00:34:05.626 --> 00:34:08.546
Our connection to nature is part of what it means to be a New Zealander.

586
00:34:09.246 --> 00:34:12.866
These places are the forests, rivers, wetlands, coastlines, and mountains where

587
00:34:12.906 --> 00:34:14.937
people go tramping, swimming, camping, and fishing.

588
00:34:15.667 --> 00:34:19.296
And as we've already seen from the incredible public response to this bill, these

589
00:34:19.306 --> 00:34:22.486
are the places that Kiwis want to protect and be able to pass on to our children

590
00:34:22.506 --> 00:34:23.187
and grandchildren.

591
00:34:24.046 --> 00:34:27.716
And in the midst of a biodiversity crisis, when we have one of the highest rates of

592
00:34:27.726 --> 00:34:31.006
species extinction in the world, around 4,000 species at risk of extinction,

593
00:34:31.546 --> 00:34:33.406
these places have never been more important.

594
00:34:34.046 --> 00:34:37.306
The biggest problem facing New Zealand's conservation system is not that it's too

595
00:34:37.326 --> 00:34:39.107
difficult to develop conservation land.

596
00:34:39.596 --> 00:34:42.016
It is that we are losing nature at an alarming rate.

597
00:34:42.667 --> 00:34:46.147
For WWF, that has to be the starting point for reform.

598
00:34:46.187 --> 00:34:49.726
The question we should be asking is how will these changes strengthen and

599
00:34:49.766 --> 00:34:52.486
improve our ability to protect and restore nature?

600
00:34:53.426 --> 00:34:56.086
And that's where our fundamental concern with this bill lies.

601
00:34:57.066 --> 00:35:00.286
Look, we do recognize there are genuine problems with the way our conservation

602
00:35:00.326 --> 00:35:03.776
system currently operates, and we do support the need to modernize the system.

603
00:35:04.926 --> 00:35:08.256
There are longstanding inefficiencies in concession processes and

604
00:35:08.746 --> 00:35:11.906
conservation management planning that have been a source of frustration for DOC and

605
00:35:11.946 --> 00:35:12.806
those who engage with it.

606
00:35:14.386 --> 00:35:17.506
And we do think there's a legitimate case for reducing unnecessary administrative

607
00:35:17.586 --> 00:35:20.856
burden, and that there are some sensible proposals in this bill to do that.

608
00:35:22.186 --> 00:35:25.406
But for us, this bill goes much further than modernizing

609
00:35:25.446 --> 00:35:29.166
processes. It fundamentally changes New Zealand's conservation system in

610
00:35:29.226 --> 00:35:31.966
ways that risk weakening longstanding protections for nature.

611
00:35:34.086 --> 00:35:37.426
This bill elevates economic development within conservation decision-making,

612
00:35:37.626 --> 00:35:41.546
increases opportunities for development on conservation land, weakens public

613
00:35:41.586 --> 00:35:43.906
participation, and reduces independent oversight.

614
00:35:45.146 --> 00:35:48.546
Ultimately, we recommend that the bill be withdrawn and substantially rewritten to

615
00:35:48.566 --> 00:35:52.206
ensure that conservation remains the overriding purpose of our conservation system.

616
00:35:53.346 --> 00:35:57.066
Our first and biggest concern is the proposed change to the purpose and

617
00:35:57.146 --> 00:35:58.746
direction of conservation management.

618
00:35:59.786 --> 00:36:03.306
The bill introduces a new function for the Department of Conservation, requiring it

619
00:36:03.346 --> 00:36:07.226
to recognize economic opportunities arising from conservation land and to

620
00:36:07.366 --> 00:36:10.726
enable that use and development to the greatest extent practicable.

621
00:36:11.646 --> 00:36:14.166
We don't think that's a minor administrative change.

622
00:36:14.226 --> 00:36:17.586
It represents a significant shift from a conservation first framework towards one

623
00:36:17.626 --> 00:36:20.866
where economic development is elevated and becomes an explicit objective of the

624
00:36:20.906 --> 00:36:21.786
conservation system.

625
00:36:23.126 --> 00:36:26.446
Economic activity can already occur on conservation land through established

626
00:36:26.486 --> 00:36:29.786
concession processes where it's consistent with conservation objectives.

627
00:36:30.386 --> 00:36:34.306
So the question is, why does economic use and development need to be elevated

628
00:36:34.326 --> 00:36:38.310
into an explicit function of DOC? We're concerned that doing so

629
00:36:38.350 --> 00:36:42.330
changes the fundamental balance of the system and risks moving us away

630
00:36:42.790 --> 00:36:45.990
from the system towards one where economic development becomes an objective in its

631
00:36:46.050 --> 00:36:49.770
own right. We're also really concerned that this provision was added in at a late

632
00:36:49.830 --> 00:36:53.490
stage and was not included in the proposals consulted on in February 2025.

633
00:36:54.410 --> 00:36:54.740
Mm.

634
00:36:54.770 --> 00:36:57.810
A change with such significant implications for the department's core functions

635
00:36:57.850 --> 00:36:59.760
should have been subject to proper public scrutiny.

636
00:37:00.530 --> 00:37:03.930
In our view, this late stage change needs to be removed in its entirety.

637
00:37:05.230 --> 00:37:07.610
I'm now going to hand over to Jamie to talk through some of our concerns.

638
00:37:07.730 --> 00:37:11.310
Great. So our second concern is the way the bill creates a more

639
00:37:11.370 --> 00:37:15.110
development-focused framework while removing important environmental

640
00:37:15.210 --> 00:37:18.770
safeguards. So the proposed pre-approved and exempt activity

641
00:37:18.810 --> 00:37:22.500
provisions would reduce scrutiny for certain activities.

642
00:37:22.510 --> 00:37:25.910
And so we understand the rationale for when you've got low risk activities,

643
00:37:26.450 --> 00:37:27.690
to be able to

644
00:37:29.730 --> 00:37:33.659
approve those more quickly. But we're concerned that the activities that have

645
00:37:33.690 --> 00:37:37.460
greater environmental impacts could also proceed without

646
00:37:37.790 --> 00:37:39.750
more robust case-by-case assessment.

647
00:37:40.390 --> 00:37:43.870
So particularly important here is the cumulative effect of multiple

648
00:37:43.890 --> 00:37:46.430
developments within these kind of areas.

649
00:37:47.250 --> 00:37:50.490
An individual activity might appear relatively minor, but obviously, when you

650
00:37:50.550 --> 00:37:54.370
multiply activities that take place over time, there are lots

651
00:37:54.410 --> 00:37:58.290
of combined effects on ecosystems, species, and habitats that can be quite

652
00:37:59.090 --> 00:38:02.750
significant. So this is particularly concerning when nature is already

653
00:38:02.810 --> 00:38:03.950
under immense pressure.

654
00:38:05.030 --> 00:38:08.750
The provisions relating to the visitor amenity areas, in particular,

655
00:38:08.850 --> 00:38:12.600
also create risks of ecosystem disturbance, habitat degradation, and

656
00:38:12.650 --> 00:38:16.110
increased pressure on already vulnerable environments.

657
00:38:16.130 --> 00:38:19.700
Again, it's the cumulative effect here that really concerns us.

658
00:38:19.770 --> 00:38:22.840
We're not arguing that every activity should be unnecessarily

659
00:38:23.190 --> 00:38:26.650
bureaucratic. We are arguing that efficiency cannot come at the

660
00:38:26.690 --> 00:38:28.770
expense of environmental bottom lines.

661
00:38:29.280 --> 00:38:33.250
So we recommend strengthening these provisions so that conservation values

662
00:38:33.310 --> 00:38:37.180
are maintained or enhanced and that independent ecological assessment

663
00:38:37.250 --> 00:38:38.490
is required where appropriate.

664
00:38:39.290 --> 00:38:43.210
We should also note that we welcome the government's stated intention to remove

665
00:38:43.230 --> 00:38:47.010
the provisions relating to the exchange and disposal of conservation land following

666
00:38:47.050 --> 00:38:48.470
the outpouring of public concern.

667
00:38:48.979 --> 00:38:52.780
Our public conservation land shouldn't be treated as a collection of bits and bobs

668
00:38:52.890 --> 00:38:56.500
that can simply be traded away. Kiwis have certainly made that known.

669
00:38:57.710 --> 00:39:00.570
So given this issue has been well traversed, we obviously won't go into further

670
00:39:00.610 --> 00:39:04.250
detail on it today, other than to say we strongly support that change and encourage

671
00:39:04.330 --> 00:39:06.810
the committee to ensure these provisions are removed from the bill.

672
00:39:07.630 --> 00:39:11.190
Our final concern is the weakening of public oversight and treaty obligations.

673
00:39:11.250 --> 00:39:15.170
The bill significantly increases the ministerial decision-making powers

674
00:39:15.550 --> 00:39:18.730
while reducing the role of the New Zealand Conservation Authority and conservation

675
00:39:18.810 --> 00:39:22.560
boards. These bodies provide important independent oversight and ecological

676
00:39:22.650 --> 00:39:24.650
expertise and community perspectives.

677
00:39:25.830 --> 00:39:29.600
Reducing them to largely advisory functions weakens the

678
00:39:29.630 --> 00:39:33.170
checks and balances and increases the risk that decisions become influenced by the

679
00:39:33.190 --> 00:39:36.360
short-term political considerations rather than long-term environmental

680
00:39:36.470 --> 00:39:36.970
stewardship.

681
00:39:37.890 --> 00:39:41.420
Public conservation land and waters are a shared national treasure, and their

682
00:39:41.490 --> 00:39:45.270
management should reflect the values and interests of those communities they

683
00:39:45.310 --> 00:39:49.290
belong to. Instead, the proposed changes would narrow the circumstances

684
00:39:49.310 --> 00:39:52.970
in which concession applications must be publicly notified, meaning

685
00:39:53.310 --> 00:39:56.930
many applications could proceed without public awareness or scrutiny.

686
00:39:56.950 --> 00:40:00.650
Public participation is further constrained by removing any automatic

687
00:40:00.730 --> 00:40:04.330
right for submitters to be heard. Hearings provide an important opportunity,

688
00:40:04.370 --> 00:40:07.610
obviously, to test evidence and ensure decision-makers hear directly from affected

689
00:40:07.670 --> 00:40:08.950
communities and stakeholders.

690
00:40:09.890 --> 00:40:13.220
We're also concerned that some of the provisions around treaty obligations and

691
00:40:13.250 --> 00:40:17.210
Māori engagement risk reducing what is currently the

692
00:40:17.290 --> 00:40:21.270
strongest obligation to give effect to treaty principles into something more like

693
00:40:21.370 --> 00:40:22.390
a procedural checklist.

694
00:40:23.390 --> 00:40:26.970
You would have heard this obviously from Matua Edward Ellison in the previous

695
00:40:26.990 --> 00:40:30.770
submission. Mātauranga Māori and Māori stewardship approaches provide

696
00:40:30.790 --> 00:40:34.290
critical insights into long-term ecosystem health and biodiversity

697
00:40:34.370 --> 00:40:37.590
protection. At its core, effective conservation

698
00:40:37.790 --> 00:40:41.600
depends on ensuring enduring partnerships and

699
00:40:41.650 --> 00:40:45.300
shared stewardship. Meaningful engagement with treaty partners is not

700
00:40:45.330 --> 00:40:48.990
simply a procedural requirement, it is fundamental to

701
00:40:49.060 --> 00:40:51.020
achieving conservation outcomes,

702
00:40:52.290 --> 00:40:55.320
and that these outcomes are durable and they're equitable, and they're

703
00:40:55.320 --> 00:40:59.150
reflective of Aotearoa's unique context.

704
00:41:00.450 --> 00:41:04.040
We therefore recommend restoring stronger notification and hearing

705
00:41:04.110 --> 00:41:06.970
rights, strengthening the roles of conservation boards and

706
00:41:07.790 --> 00:41:11.620
NZCA, and ensuring early, meaningful, and properly resourced iwi and

707
00:41:11.650 --> 00:41:15.110
hapū engagement with greater opportunities for Māori leadership in

708
00:41:15.190 --> 00:41:16.550
conservation decision-making.

709
00:41:17.550 --> 00:41:20.790
Ultimately, we think the conservation system works best when decisions are informed

710
00:41:20.830 --> 00:41:24.560
by strong science, independent oversight, public participation, and

711
00:41:24.610 --> 00:41:26.370
genuine partnership with Tangata Whenua.

712
00:41:27.550 --> 00:41:31.330
To conclude, WWF agrees that Aotearoa, New Zealand's conservation system needs

713
00:41:31.410 --> 00:41:35.210
modernization. We support better processes, greater efficiency,

714
00:41:35.370 --> 00:41:39.330
and removing unnecessary barriers to effective conservation management.

715
00:41:39.850 --> 00:41:41.700
But this bill goes much further than that.

716
00:41:42.190 --> 00:41:45.260
So it changes the direction of our conservation system in ways that

717
00:41:46.350 --> 00:41:48.320
risk weakening protections for nature.

718
00:41:50.690 --> 00:41:53.910
As Caitlin mentioned, at a time when we're facing a biodiversity crisis, we need

719
00:41:53.950 --> 00:41:57.560
reforms that actually strengthen our ability to protect and restore nature, not

720
00:41:57.590 --> 00:42:01.010
make it easier to develop the places we're already struggling to protect.

721
00:42:01.670 --> 00:42:05.630
Reform of this magnitude must be grounded in a clear and unwavering commitment

722
00:42:05.690 --> 00:42:09.600
to protect our biodiversity, maintaining ecological integrity, and upholding

723
00:42:09.630 --> 00:42:13.590
the public interest. The decisions here will determine what is left for the next

724
00:42:13.610 --> 00:42:17.150
generation. The rivers, the forests, the wetlands, the

725
00:42:17.190 --> 00:42:20.980
coastlines, and mountains we protect today are the places future generations

726
00:42:21.030 --> 00:42:23.410
should still be able to know and explore and love.

727
00:42:24.750 --> 00:42:28.679
WWF urges the committee to withdraw and substantially rewrite this bill, so that

728
00:42:28.710 --> 00:42:32.600
instead of weakening our conservation framework, it creates the strong,

729
00:42:32.810 --> 00:42:36.580
modern system Aotearoa needs to halt and reverse biodiversity loss and

730
00:42:36.610 --> 00:42:38.530
restore our natural environment.

731
00:42:39.100 --> 00:42:41.290
Thank you for your time today, and we welcome any questions.

732
00:42:42.406 --> 00:42:46.245
Thank you very much for your submission. Look, we'll go first to Grant McCallum.

733
00:42:46.306 --> 00:42:47.926
Yeah. Morning, great to see you both.

734
00:42:48.006 --> 00:42:50.266
I want to touch on the

735
00:42:51.666 --> 00:42:55.226
whole land sale and disposal discussion that created a lot of

736
00:42:56.186 --> 00:42:58.026
angst, you might say. Do you

737
00:42:58.906 --> 00:43:02.026
feel there's any place at all for the DOC to be able to

738
00:43:02.966 --> 00:43:05.166
sell and/or swap land?

739
00:43:06.406 --> 00:43:09.756
Of course. There's already provisions that do that.

740
00:43:09.786 --> 00:43:09.976
Mm-hmm.

741
00:43:10.246 --> 00:43:13.606
